public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // Check if n is less than or equal to zero
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // Check if n is divisible by 3
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3; // Divide n by 3
        }

        // If n is not divisible by 3, then it cannot be a power of three
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

This code checks if `n` is less than or equal to zero and returns `false` in that case. Then, it enters a loop that continues until `n` becomes 1. In each iteration, it divides `n` by 3 using the modulus operator (`%`). This process repeats until `n` becomes 1, indicating that `n` is a power of three. If at any point `n` is not 1 after the division, it means `n` is not a power of three, so the function returns `false`. Otherwise, it returns `true`.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log(n)), as we divide `n` by 3 in each iteration until it reaches 1. The space complexity is also O(1), as we only use a constant amount of extra space to store the intermediate values.

